  "synonym_discrimination": null,
  "usage_note": "Shit is one of the most common vulgar words in English. It is heard often in casual speech among friends and in films, but it offends many people and is not used in polite or formal situations, at work with people you do not know well, or in careful writing. A learner is safest learning to recognize it rather than to use it. The neutral words are excrement for the waste, nonsense for sense 2, and stuff for sense 3.",
  "learner_errors": [],
  "etymology": null,
  "see_also": [],
  "adaptation": {
    "semantic": "Other languages usually have an equally vulgar word for the literal sense, but the figurative senses (nonsense, things, a hated person) and the many idioms rarely translate word for word. Each needs the nearest vulgar or informal expression in the target language, matched for strength.",
    "grammar": "Uncountable in the literal sense and in the senses nonsense and things; countable for a hated person: a shit, two shits. Give a shit is almost always negative: not give a shit.",
    "culture": "The word is common in films and in casual American speech, which can make it sound milder than it is. It is still cut from much television, avoided in front of children and at work, and rare in print. Its strength varies between English-speaking countries and between generations.",
    "false_friends": null,
    "pronunciation": "Rhymes with hit and sit, with a short i. Learners whose languages lack the short i may say sheet instead, which is a different word: keep the vowel short."
  },
